# Line endings are declared here rather than left to `core.autocrlf`, because
# that setting is per-machine and this repo has three consumers that disagree
# about what the WORKING TREE should contain:
#
#   * .editorconfig       -- `end_of_line = lf` for everything, overridden to
#                            crlf for PowerShell and batch scripts.
#   * .pre-commit-config  -- `mixed-line-ending --fix=lf`, excluding the same
#                            script types plus .psc.
#   * the Creation Kit    -- authors and expects CRLF in .psc sources.
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` and no rules here, git wrote CRLF into every file
# on checkout while the pre-commit hook rewrote those same files to LF and
# failed the commit. The hook's own stash/restore then re-converted them, so
# it could never converge: any commit that left something unstaged aborted,
# every time, and the only way through was --no-verify.
#
# Everything below normalises to LF in the repository regardless; `eol=`
# controls only what lands in the working tree. These rules take precedence
# over core.autocrlf, so the behaviour is now identical on every machine.

# Default: text detected automatically, LF in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch keep CRLF in the working tree, matching .editorconfig.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Papyrus is Creation-Kit-authored and edited in CK tooling that expects CRLF.
# The pre-commit hook excludes .psc for the same reason.
*.psc  text eol=crlf

# Plugin files are binary and go through Git LFS; never touch their contents.
*.esl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.esp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.esm filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.seq filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
